#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506638} :activity/id #uuid "442f7906-7af4-5d3c-b0ed-f94af1daddc5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a90ff20b-46bc-563c-8a6d-2698d67747fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316068} :answer-field/id #uuid "49330cc6-9572-5a0d-b1f2-3e73f8b2ef51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d4ef002-5026-5196-8b56-541878d83ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343372" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=-2(-3)+1=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "589a89f1-3670-5a97-9173-deba2449c324" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a066781a-6e52-5220-93ef-23ff199bd9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f46bb7-8b99-5788-8b85-2ae0679bb181" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc552628-2cac-58c1-ac79-840e54e9a1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-343390" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4, & x\\in [-5,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=(-3)^{2}-4=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "763f5bea-94fa-514d-8f84-2fa7e7e9d8bd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088118d9-d82e-5555-ae2d-6b6c94d1c0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391815} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc13dfa-2ecd-5239-9fbb-48bd4bb71145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391816} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1eb88d-b598-57b0-9201-7890985133cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391817} {:answer/id #uuid "1eddfbd5-9f38-5c59-b2d4-5ed1bfea29b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391816} :answer-field/id #uuid "74dce7e8-8cd9-587e-b02b-722f57c4a1c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5fa43ec-082f-5051-8bd2-f2697aaabdbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391820} {:answer/id #uuid "d1477410-5650-553a-8e7e-4f722e3de5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391821} {:answer/id #uuid "86d236a1-c815-5c11-9ff9-24c7f3e6f09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391822} {:answer/id #uuid "304ead85-1477-5158-9c81-f158cc755a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391820} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80d872f-e8aa-5257-8cb5-be49497a6b44" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391819} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3721b96f-c770-5ed4-b0bb-886380288864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391825} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee6cd9d-926e-5f7e-80f3-b512fbbfd9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391826} {:answer/id #uuid "6242ee14-aa46-52e1-9ea4-3919fdb9fd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391827} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e3c3e8-cafb-59da-905f-3dfdbc9e7c24" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5889a416-25b2-5826-9187-131a2ada5c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391829} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f8e8b6-6e4f-5f2a-a120-02c5bf806f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391829} :answer-field/id #uuid "50452121-b236-57c2-988c-53f0a2c87054" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f28129a8-5e3e-5ca3-8128-6017fbcdb672" :question/math-academy-id "q-342573" :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (2n-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "953b546a-265d-5b9a-a212-47ea85583cca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59e944d4-7bfb-518e-9bee-2fdf150f8144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402322} {:answer/id #uuid "47de1570-9f8f-50d0-b18a-c75ae926d19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402323} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcea2a2-3096-50e6-a